     "Cold resistance (TP-6844 p.15, read under the 24V/36V/48V column headings): 0.002 / 0.005 / 0.009 ohm. Most ohmmeters cannot read below 1 ohm, so continuity with no discoloration counts as good.",
     "Continuity on a good stator (TP-6844 p.73, Figure 61). Pairs 1-2, 2-3, 3-1, 4-5, 5-6, 6-4, 1-4, 2-5, 3-6, 1-5, 2-6, 3-4, 1-6, 2-4, 3-5 should all show continuity (the table has one merged 'Yes' cell for every pair row). No lead should show continuity to ground. Leads 4-6 exist on 24V units only; 36/48V units have output leads 1, 2, 3.",
     "CAUTION on the resistance column order: TP-6844 p.15 row labels run 48V/36V/24V, but the column headings run 24V/36V/48V. The breaker values in that same table are the reverse of spec sheet G4-213 (see tips). Treat the per-voltage resistance values as uncertain; a near-zero reading with no discoloration passes.",
     "Megger: apply 500 VDC between each lead and the frame. About 500 kOhm (1/2 megohm) or more is good; less means replace or repair (TP-6844 p.73)."
